theory.intro:
Two lines are **perpendicular** when they meet (or cross) to form a right angle of $90^{\circ}$.

[tikz]\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large},scale=0.9]
\usetikzlibrary{calc}
\draw (-2.2,0) node[left] {$l$} -- (2.2,0);
\draw (0,-2.2) node[below] {$m$} -- (0,2.2);
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
\coordinate (X) at ($(O)!0.35cm!(2.2,0)$);
\coordinate (Y) at ($(O)!0.35cm!(0,2.2)$);
\draw (X)--($(X)+(Y)-(O)$)--(Y);
\end{tikzpicture}[/tikz]

foundation.question_text:
Are lines $AB$ and $CD$ perpendicular?
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large},scale=0.9]
\draw (-2,0) node[left] {$A$}--(2,0) node[right] {$B$}; \draw (0,-2) node[below] {$C$}--(0,2) node[above] {$D$};
\coordinate (R) at (0,0); \coordinate (X) at (0.35,0); \coordinate (Y) at (0,0.35); \draw (X)--(0.35,0.35)--(Y);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Decide whether lines $EF$ and $GH$ are perpendicular.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large},scale=0.9]
\draw (-2,-0.6) node[left] {$E$}--(2,0.6) node[right] {$F$}; \draw (-1.5,1.7) node[above] {$G$}--(1.5,-1.7) node[below] {$H$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Write the perpendicular statement shown.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large},scale=0.9]
\usetikzlibrary{calc}
\draw (-2,-1) node[left] {$P$}--(2,1) node[right] {$Q$}; \draw (-1,2) node[above] {$R$}--(1,-2) node[below] {$S$};
\coordinate (O) at (0,0); \coordinate (A) at ($(O)!0.3cm!(2,1)$); \coordinate (B) at ($(O)!0.3cm!(-1,2)$); \draw (A)--($(A)+(B)-(O)$)--(B);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

A shelf support meets a vertical post as shown.
Are the two edges perpendicular?
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large},scale=0.9]
\draw[very thick] (-2,0)--(2,0); \draw[very thick] (0,-1.8)--(0,2);
\draw (0.35,0)--(0.35,0.35)--(0,0.35);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Which feature proves that $KL$ is perpendicular to $MN$?
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large},scale=0.9]
\draw (-2,0) node[left] {$K$}--(2,0) node[right] {$L$}; \draw (0,-2) node[below] {$M$}--(0,2) node[above] {$N$};
\draw (0.3,0)--(0.3,0.3)--(0,0.3);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

True or false: the two roads shown are perpendicular.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large},scale=0.9]
\draw[double,double distance=3pt] (-2.3,-0.8)--(2.3,0.8); \draw[double,double distance=3pt] (-1.6,1.8)--(1.6,-1.8);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

development.question_text:
Lines $a$ and $b$ meet at $90^{\circ}$.
Complete the statement $a\;?\;b$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large},scale=0.9]
\usetikzlibrary{calc}
\draw (-2.2,-0.7)--(2.2,0.7) node[right] {$a$}; \draw (-0.7,2.2)--(0.7,-2.2) node[below] {$b$};
\coordinate (O) at (0,0); \coordinate (A) at ($(O)!0.3cm!(2.2,0.7)$); \coordinate (B) at ($(O)!0.3cm!(-0.7,2.2)$); \draw (A)--($(A)+(B)-(O)$)--(B);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

A learner says any two intersecting lines are perpendicular.
Use the figure to test the claim.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large},scale=0.9]
\draw (-2.3,-0.4)--(2.3,0.4); \draw (-1.6,-1.7)--(1.6,1.7);
\node at (0.7,0.75) {$65^{\circ}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Are opposite sides $AB$ and $CD$ perpendicular in this rectangle?
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large},scale=0.9]
\draw (0,0) node[below left] {$A$}--(3,0) node[below right] {$B$}--(3,1.7) node[above right] {$C$}--(0,1.7) node[above left] {$D$}--cycle;
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Name one pair of perpendicular sides in the rectangle.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large},scale=0.9]
\draw (0,0) node[below left] {$W$}--(3.2,0) node[below right] {$X$}--(3.2,1.8) node[above right] {$Y$}--(0,1.8) node[above left] {$Z$}--cycle;
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Two line segments do not touch in the drawing.
Could their full lines be perpendicular?
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large},scale=0.9]
\draw (-2,-1)--(-0.4,-1); \draw (0.8,-0.2)--(0.8,1.6);
\draw[dashed] (-0.4,-1)--(0.8,-1)--(0.8,-0.2);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

The corner of a triangular sign is marked as shown.
Are sides $UV$ and $UW$ perpendicular?
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large},scale=0.9]
\usetikzlibrary{calc}
\coordinate (U) at (0,0); \coordinate (V) at (3,0); \coordinate (W) at (0,2.3);
\draw (U) node[below left] {$U$}--(V) node[below right] {$V$}--(W) node[above left] {$W$}--cycle;
\coordinate (A) at ($(U)!0.3cm!(V)$); \coordinate (B) at ($(U)!0.3cm!(W)$); \draw (A)--($(A)+(B)-(U)$)--(B);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
